so \u00d710=4020. Then 67\u00d75 is 335. Adding gives 4355. So that's correct. \n\nWait, perhaps they thought 67\u00d765 is 60\u00d765 +7\u00d765. 60\u00d765 is 3900, 7\u00d765 is 455, so total 4355. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 67\u00d765 as 65\u00d767, but did 6\u00d76=36, 6\u00d77=42, 5\u00d76=30, 5\u00d77=35, then added those numbers: 36+42+30+35= 143. Then multiplied by something? Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 67\u00d765 as (60\u00d765) + (7\u00d765). If they miscalculated 60\u00d765 as 3600, but 6\u00d765 is 390, so 60\u00d765 is 3900. Correct. 7\u00d765=455. 3900+455=4355. So correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 67\u00d765 is (70\u00d760) + (something). 70\u00d760=4200, but then added 7\u00d75? 4200+35=4235, which is different. Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they added 67+65 first, got 132, then multiplied by 70? 132\u00d770=9240. Not 9355. Close, but not exactly. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps they did 67\u00d765 as 67\u00d770 -67\u00d75. 67\u00d770 is 4690, minus 335 is 4355. Correct. \n\nHmm, I can't figure out how they got 9355. Maybe they thought 67\u00d765 is 67\u00d76\u00d710 + 67\u00d75, but miscalculated 6\u00d767 as 402, but then added 402 and 335 to get 737, then multiplied by 10? 737\u00d710 is 7370, which is still not 9355. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 67\u00d765 as 6\u00d76\u00d7100 + (7\u00d75)\u00d7100, but that's 36\u00d7100 +35\u00d7100= 39500. No. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 67\u00d765 = (60\u00d760) + (7\u00d75) + (60\u00d75\u00d72). Let me see: 3600 +35 + 600 = 4235. Not matching. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 67\u00d765 as (60\u00d765) + (7\u00d765). But if they thought 60\u00d765 is 3600 (which is wrong), then 3600+455=4055. Not 9355. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 67 by 65 as (70\u00d765) - (3\u00d765). 70\u00d765 is 4550, minus 195 is 4355. So correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 67\u00d765 is 65\u00d765 +65\u00d72. 65\u00d765 is 4225, plus 130 is 4355. Correct. \n\nHmm. I'm really not sure how they got 9355. Let me try multiplying 67\u00d795, which would be higher. 67\u00d795 is 6365. Still not 9355. \n\nWait, 67\u00d7140 is 9380. Close to 9355 but not exactly. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 67\u00d765 as 67\u00d7(100-35). 67\u00d7100=6700 minus 67\u00d735. 67\u00d735 is 2345. 6700-2345=4355. Correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they added 67 and 65 to get 132, then multiplied by 70.5? 132\u00d770.5 = 132\u00d770 +132\u00d70.5 = 9240 +66=9306. Not 9355. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps they multiplied 67\u00d765 as 67\u00d76\u00d710 +67\u00d75\u00d710. That would be (402\u00d710)+(335\u00d710)=4020+3350=7370. Still no. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 67\u00d765 as (6\u00d76) concatenated with (7\u00d75)= 36 and 35, so 3635? No. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 67\u00d765 by adding 67 sixty-five times. Let's see:\n\n67\u00d72=134, \u00d73=201, \u00d74=268, \u00d75=335, \u00d710=670, \u00d720=1340, \u00d730=2010, \u00d750=3350. \n\n65 is 50+15. So 3350 + (67\u00d715). 67\u00d710=670, \u00d75=335, so 67\u00d715=1005. So total 3350 +1005=4355. Correct. \n\nHmm. So all methods lead to 4355. The user must have made an error in their calculation. Therefore, the correct product is 4355. So the assistant needs to correct that.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 67 and 65 is calculated as follows:\n\nUsing the standard multiplication method:\n```\n    67\n  \u00d7 65\n  -----\n    335  (67 \u00d7 5)\n  4020   (67 \u00d7 60, shifted one position to the left)\n  -----\n   4355\n```\n\nVerification using the distributive property:\n\\[\n(60 + 7)(60 + 5) = 60 \\times 60 + 60 \\times 5 + 7 \\times 60 + 7 \\times 5 = 3600 + 300 + 420 + 35 = 4355\n\\]\n\nAnother method using the difference of squares:\n\\[\n67 \\times 65 = (66 + 1)(66 - 1) = 66^2 - 1 = 4356 - 1 = 4355\n\\]\n\n**Final Answer:** The product of 67 and 65 is **4355**.\n\nThe initial thought of 9355 is incorrect. The correct result after careful calculation is **\\boxed{4355}**.",
