e user's thought of 3791 is incorrect? Alternatively, did I make a mistake here? Let me do the multiplication directly:\n\n91 multiplied by 41. Let me do it step by step:\n\n91 \u00d7 41. Let's multiply 91 by 40 first, which we did before as 3640, and then add 91. So 3640 +91 is 3731. Alternatively, if I use a calculator, but since I can't use a calculator, perhaps another way is to use the difference of squares or another trick?\n\nWait, 91 is 90 +1, and 41 is 40 +1. Alternatively, maybe 91 \u00d7 41 can be thought of as (90+1)*(40+1). Wait, which is exactly what I did before, leading to 3731.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought it was (90+1)*(40+1) but miscalculated. Let me see:\n\nSuppose someone does (90 \u00d740)= 3600, then adds (90 \u00d71)=90 and (1\u00d740)=40, which totals 3600 +90+40= 3730, then adds the 1\u00d71=1, making 3731. But perhaps they added 90+40 as 130 instead of 130? Wait, 90+40 is 130, so 3600 +130 is 3730 plus 1 is 3731. So that's correct. Alternatively, maybe they thought 90 \u00d741 plus 1\u00d741? Let's see:\n\n90 \u00d741. Let's see, 90\u00d740=3600, plus 90\u00d71=90, so 3600+90=3690. Then add 1\u00d741=41, so total is 3690+41=3731. So same result.\n\nHmm, so all methods lead to 3731. So the user's answer of 3791 is incorrect. Perhaps they added 3640 + 150 instead of 91? 3640 +150 would be 3790, and then adding 1 more? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe they did 91 \u00d7 40 = 3640, then added 91 \u00d71 =91, but mistakenly added 3640 + 150 (maybe miscalculating 91 as 150?), which would give 3790. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps they thought 91 \u00d7 40 = 3640, then added 91\u00d71=91, but when adding 3640 + 91, they did 3640 + 100 = 3740, then subtracted 9 to get 3731. But if they added 3640 + 91 as 3640 + 90 = 3730 +1 = 3731. Wait, maybe they added wrong? Like 3640 + 91 as 3640 + 90 = 3730, plus 1 is 3731. So unless they made an arithmetic mistake here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 91 \u00d741 as (90 \u00d740) + (90 \u00d