+91=3731. But if they instead did 91\u00d740 as (90\u00d740)+(90\u00d71)=3600+90=3690, then added 1\u00d741=41, totaling 3690+41=3731. So all methods still get 3731. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is 3731. The user's thought of 3791 must be incorrect. Perhaps they made an arithmetic mistake in their calculation. So the assistant should explain the correct method and point out the mistake.\n</think>\n\nThe correct product of 91 and 41 is **3,731**, not 3,791. Here's the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n1. **Break down the multiplication using the distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   91 \\times 41 = 91 \\times (40 + 1) = (91 \\times 40) + (91 \\times 1)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Calculate each part separately**:\n   - \\( 91 \\times 40 = 3,\\!640 \\)  \n     *(Because \\( 91 \\times 4 = 364 \\), then multiply by 10 for the 40.)*\n   - \\( 91 \\times 1 = 91 \\)\n\n3. **Add the results**:\n   \\[\n   3,\\!640 + 91 = 3,\\!731\n   \\]\n\n### Why 3,791 is incorrect:\n- If someone miscalculates \\( 91 \\times 40 \\) as \\( 3,\\!700 \\) instead of \\( 3,\\!640 \\), adding 91 would give \\( 3,\\!791 \\).  \n- Alternatively, a simple arithmetic error during addition (e.g., \\( 3,\\!640 + 91 = 3,\\!731 \\), not \\( 3,\\!791 \\)) could lead to this mistake.\n\n### Final Answer:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3731}\n\\]",
