ts. So:\n\nFirst cell (top 1, side 3): 1\u00d73=03\nSecond cell (top 1, side 1): 1\u00d71=01\nThird cell (top 6, side 3):6\u00d73=18\nFourth cell (top 6, side 1):6\u00d71=06\n\nThen split each cell into diagonals. The top right of each cell goes to the upper right, and the bottom left goes to the lower left. Then add along the diagonals. Let me visualize this.\n\nThe grid would look like this:\n\n\u2003\u00a01\u20036\n3\u2003| 3 | 18\n\u2003\u00a0|------- \n1\u2003| 1 | 6\n\nWait, maybe I need to structure it properly. Let me try again. The lattice method grid for multiplying 16 \u00d7 31 would have two rows and two columns. The top number (16) is split into 1 and 6 along the top, and the right number (31) is split into 3 and 1 along the right side. Each cell is divided by a diagonal line.\n\nStarting with the first cell (top-left): 1 \u00d7 3 = 3. So write 0 in the top right and 3 in the bottom left? Wait, no, actually, each product is split into tens and units. Since 1\u00d73=03, the tens digit (0) goes to the upper left triangle and the units (3) go to the lower right. Wait, maybe I need to recall the exact steps.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the lattice method is overcomplicating here. Let me stick with standard multiplication. Since both methods give me 496, that's solid.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user added 16 + 30 first? Like 16\u00d730 is 480, then plus 16 gives 496. So that's correct. Alternatively, if they thought 16\u00d730 is 500? Then 500 + 96 would be 596, but that's incorrect. So maybe they miscalculated 16\u00d730 as 500 instead of 480. Yeah, 16\u00d730 is 480. So adding 16 gives 496.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 16 \u00d7 31 as (10 +6)(30 +1) and expanded that. Let's see:\n\n(10+6)(30+1) = 10\u00d730 +10\u00d71 +6\u00d730 +6\u00d71 = 300 + 10 + 180 +6 = 300+10 is 310, 310+180 is 490, plus 6 is 496. Yep, same answer.\n\nTherefore, the correct product is 496, and the user's thought of 596 is incorrect. So I should explain that the correct answer is