0\u00d780 as 160 instead of 1600, but that would be way off. Maybe they thought 27\u00d784 is 27\u00d780 plus 27\u00d74, but miscalculated 27\u00d74 as 105 instead of 108? Then 2160+105=2265, still not 2263. Alternatively, 27\u00d74=104? That would be 2264. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe they added 2160 + 108 as 2268 but thought the answer was 2263, perhaps a typo? Maybe they thought 2263 because they did 2268 minus 5. Not sure, but regardless, my calculation shows 2268. \n\nAlternatively, let me do the multiplication in another way. Let's think of 84\u00d727. \n\nBreakdown:\n\n84 \u00d7 27  \n= 84 \u00d7 (20 + 7)  \n= 84\u00d720 + 84\u00d77  \n= 1680 + 588  \n= 2268. \n\nYes, that's still the same. 84\u00d720 is 1680. 84\u00d77: 80\u00d77=560 and 4\u00d77=28, so total 588. 1680+588 is 2268. \n\nTherefore, I'm confident the correct answer is 2268. The user might have made an arithmetic mistake in their calculation leading them to 2263, but the correct product is 2268.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 27 and 84 is \\boxed{2268}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 27 and 84, we can use the distributive property and break down the numbers into smaller parts:\n\n\\[\n(20 + 7) \\times (80 + 4)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n20 \\times 80 + 20 \\times 4 + 7 \\times 80 + 7 \\times 4\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n20 \\times 80 = 1600\n\\]\n\\[\n20 \\times 4 = 80\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 80 = 560\n\\]\n\\[\n7 \\times 4 = 28\n\\]\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\n\\[\n1600 + 80 + 560 + 28\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(1600 + 80\\):\n\n\\[\n1600 + 80 = 1680\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(1680 + 560\\):\n\n\\[\n1680 + 560 = 2240\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(2240 + 28\\):\n\n\\[\n2240 + 28 = 2268\n\\]\n\nWe can also verify this using the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   27 \\\\\n\\times 84 \\\\\n\\hline\n  108 \\quad (\\text{27 \u00d7 4}) \\\\\n+2160 \\quad (\\text{27 \u00d7 80, shifted one position to the left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  2268 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 27 and 84 is \\(\\boxed{2268}\\). The user's thought of 2263 is incorrect, likely due to an arithmetic error in their calculation.",
