174. If they thought 2\u00d78 was 15, then 15+1=16, so 16 in the tens place, making the first partial product 164? Then adding 164 +6090 would be 6254. Not 6269.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 8\u00d77=56, then wrote that as 5600, but added something else wrong. Wait, this is getting too convoluted. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 87 multiplied by 72 as (90 -3)*(70 +2) = 90*70 +90*2 -3*70 -3*2 = 6300 + 180 -210 -6 = 6300+180=6480 -210=6270 -6=6264. Still same.\n\nHmm, I'm getting 6264 every time. The user's answer was 6269, which is 5 more. Maybe they added 5 by mistake somewhere. Alternatively, maybe they thought 8\u00d77 is 58 or something? Wait, 8\u00d77 is 56. If someone thought it was 58, then in the second partial product when multiplying 7\u00d78 (the 8 in 87), they get 58 instead of 56. Then adding the carryover 4: 58+4=62, so the partial product would be 629 (since 7\u00d77 was 49, so 9 and carryover 4, then 7\u00d78=58 plus 4 is 62, so writing 62 in front, making 629, shifted to 6290. Then adding 6290 +174: 6290+100=6390+74=6464. Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, in the first partial product, if they thought 2\u00d78 is 17 (instead of 16), so 2\u00d78=16, plus carryover 1 would be 17. Then the first partial product would be 174, which is correct. Not seeing where the 5 comes from.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user multiplied 87 by 73 instead of 72? Let's check 87\u00d773. 87\u00d770=6090, 87\u00d73=261, total 6090+261=6351. Not 6269.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 87\u00d772 as (80\u00d772) + (7\u00d772). Let's see:\n\n80\u00d772: 80\u00d770=5600, 80\u00d72=160, total 5760.\n\n7\u00d772=504. So total 5760+504=6264. Same result again.\n\nHmm, so I can't figure out why the user thought it was 6269. Unless they added the partial products incorrectly. Let me check 174 + 6090 again:\n\n6090\n\n+ 174\n\n=?\n\nStarting from the right:\n\n0 +4 =4\n\n9 +7 =16 \u2192 write 6, carryover 1\n\n0 +1 +1 (carryover) =2\n\n6 +0=6\n\nSo the total is 6264. \n\nWait, unless