f 81? Then times 10 would be 820, but that's still not 812. Alternatively, maybe they did 3\u00d728 which is 84, leading to 840, but that's not either. Hmm. Anyway, regardless of the possible errors, the correct product is 810. \n\nSo the final answer should be 810. I need to make sure I present that clearly.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 30 and 27 is calculated as follows:  \n\\[\n30 \\times 27 = 30 \\times (20 + 7) = (30 \\times 20) + (30 \\times 7) = 600 + 210 = 810\n\\]  \nAlternatively, breaking it down:  \n\\[\n30 \\times 27 = (3 \\times 10) \\times 27 = 3 \\times 27 \\times 10 = 81 \\times 10 = 810\n\\]  \nBoth methods confirm the correct answer is **810**. The initial guess of 812 contains an error in calculation.  \n\n**Final Answer:** 810",
