0 + 171 = 1881 again. So regardless of the method, it's 1881. Therefore, the user's answer of 1801 is incorrect. \n\nTherefore, the correct product of 19 and 99 is 1881, not 1801. I should explain that clearly.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 19 and 99 is \\boxed{1881}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 19 and 99, we can use the distributive property by recognizing that 99 is 100 minus 1. \n\nFirst, we rewrite the problem using this observation:\n\\[\n19 \\times 99 = 19 \\times (100 - 1)\n\\]\n\nNext, we apply the distributive property:\n\\[\n19 \\times (100 - 1) = 19 \\times 100 - 19 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[\n19 \\times 100 = 1900\n\\]\n\\[\n19 \\times 1 = 19\n\\]\n\nSubtracting these results:\n\\[\n1900 - 19 = 1881\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can also perform the multiplication directly:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   19 \\\\\n\\times 99 \\\\\n\\hline\n   171 \\quad (\\text{from } 19 \\times 9) \\\\\n+1710 \\quad (\\text{from } 19 \\times 90, \\text{ shifted one position left}) \\\\\n\\hline\n  1881 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 19 and 99 is \\boxed{1881}. The initial thought of 1801 was incorrect due to a miscalculation in the subtraction step.",
