5 (which is 95), totaling 1425, but maybe they added wrong? 1330 + 95: 1330 + 100 is 1430, minus 5 is 1425. So that's correct. \n\nWait, maybe they thought 19 times 75 is (20*75) minus (1*75). So if someone thought 20*75 is 1500, then subtracting 75 gives 1425. But if they thought 20*75 is 1500 but added instead of subtracted? 1500 +75 would be 1575, which is still not 1725. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought of 19*75 as 19*(70+5) = 19*70 +19*5. If they miscalcuated 19*70 as 1400 (instead of 1330), then 1400 +95 would be 1495, still not 1725. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe they thought 19*75 = (20 + (-1)) *75, but messed up signs. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps they did 20*75 which is 1500, then added 75 instead of subtracting, getting 1500+75=1575, but that's not 1725. Alternatively, maybe they multiplied 19 by 90 (19*90=1710) and thought that was 75? \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought of 19*75 as 19 multiplied by 75, but miscalculated the multiplication steps. Let me see:\n\nSuppose someone does 19 times 75 as:\n\nFirst, 19*70 = 1330 (correct)\n\nThen 19*5 = 95 (correct)\n\nAdding 1330 +95: 1330 + 95 is 1425. So unless they added 1330 + 95 as 1425, but maybe they did 1330 + 95 as 1425, so that's right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 19 times 75 is 19 times 70 (1330) plus 19 times 5 (95), but added 1330 + 95 as 1425, but the user thought of a different sum? Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought of 19 multiplied by 75 as (25*19)*3. Since 75 is 25*3. Let's see, 25*19 is 475, then 475*3 is 1425. So that also gives the same. \n\nHmm, perhaps the user had an arithmetic error. Alternatively, maybe the question was different? Like 29*75 or 19*85? Let me check 19*85: 20*85=1700 minus 1*85=85, so 1700-85=1615. Not 1725. 25*75=1875, 20*85=1700. 19*90=1710. Hmm, maybe 25*75 is 1875, which is over. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 19*75 as 10*75 +9*75. 10*75=750, 9*75=675, so 750+675=1425. Yep. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought of 19*75 as 19*(70 +5)