Hundreds:1 +1 =2\n\nThousands:2\n\nSo total is 2272. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe when adding, they did 142 +2130 as 142 + 2100 +30 = 142+2100 is 2242, plus 30 is 2272. Still the same.\n\nHmm. So perhaps the user made a mistake in their calculation, thinking that 32*70 is 2912 or something? Wait, 32*70 is 2240. Alternatively, if they thought 32*70 is (30*70) + (2*70) but miscalculated 30*70 as 2100, which is right, plus 2*70=140, totaling 2240. So that's correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 32*71 as (30*70)+(2*1). That would be 2100 +2 =2102, which is wrong. But that's a different error.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they added 32 and 71 first, getting 103, then multiplied by something? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 32 by 72 instead of 71? 32*70=2240, plus 32*2=64, so 2240+64=2304. Still not 2972.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 32*70 as (30*70) + (2*70) = 2100 +140 =2240, then added 32*10 instead of 32*1. 2240 + 320 =2560. Still not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 32*71 is 32*7*11, since 71 is 7*10 +1, but that complicates it more. Let me see:\n\n32*7=224, then 224*11. 224*10=2240, plus 224*1=224, totaling 2240+224=2464. Still not 2972.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, maybe they did 32*70 as 32 multiplied by 7 and then by 10, but miscalculated 32*7 as 224, which is correct. So 224*10=2240, then adding 32 gives 2272. So unless they added 2240 + 732 or something, but that seems random.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 71 is 70 +1, so 32*(70+1) = 32*70 +32*1, but they miscalculated 32*70 as 2240? Wait, that's correct. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 32 multiplied by 70 is 32 times 7 times 10, but they thought 32*7 is something else. Let me check what 32*7 is. 30*7=210, 2*7=14, so 210+14=224. So that's right. So 224*10 is 2240. Then adding 32 gives 2272. So unless they thought 32*7 is 224, but then multiplied by 10 again? 224*10*10? That would be 22400, but that's way off.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps they thought 32 times 71 i