58 is close to 60, and 65 is 5 more than 60. Alternatively, maybe think of 58\u00d765 as (60 - 2)(60 + 5). Not sure if that helps, but let's try expanding that.\n\n(60 - 2)(60 +5) = 60\u00d760 + 60\u00d75 - 2\u00d760 -2\u00d75 = 3600 + 300 -120 -10. Let's compute that:\n\n3600 + 300 = 3900\n\n3900 -120 = 3780\n\n3780 -10 = 3770. Again the same result. So that must be correct.\n\nHmm, so perhaps the user thought 58\u00d760 is 3480, then 58\u00d75 is 290, adding them gives 3480+290=3770. So their answer of 3570 is wrong. Maybe they did 58\u00d760 as 3480, and then 58\u00d75 as 290, but then subtracted instead of adding? Or miscalculated one of the terms?\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 50\u00d760=3000, 50\u00d75=250, 8\u00d760=480, and 8\u00d75=40, but added them as 3000+250=3250, plus 480 is 3730, plus 40 is 3770. So their mistake might be in adding the terms incorrectly. For instance, if they thought 3000+250+480= 3000+250 is 3250, plus 480 is 3730, then plus 40 is 3770. So unless they added wrong steps, but the correct answer is indeed 3770.\n\nSo I think the correct answer is 3770, not 3570. Therefore, the user might have made an error in their calculation. Let me confirm once more using another method. Let me do 58\u00d765 on calculator-style multiplication.\n\n58 multiplied by 65:\n\nFirst, 58 \u00d7 60 = 3480\n\n58 \u00d75 = 290\n\nAdding those together: 3480 +290 = 3770. Yep. So the correct answer should be 3770. Therefore, the user's initial thought of 3570 is incorrect. Maybe they miscalculated 58\u00d760 as 3500? Let me see, 58\u00d760 is 58\u00d76\u00d710. 58\u00d76 is 348, so 3480. So if they thought 58\u00d76 is 350, that would give 3500. Then adding 290 would get 3790, but maybe they messed up in another way. Anyway, the correct answer is 3770.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 58 and 65 is \\boxed{3770}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 58 and 65, we can use the distributive property by breaking down the numbers into their place values:\n\n\\[\n58 \\times 65 = (50 + 8) \\times (60 + 5)\n\\]\n\nExpanding this using the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(50 + 8) \\times (60 + 5) = 50 \\times 60 + 50 \\times 5 + 8 \\times 60 + 8 \\times 5\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n50 \\times 60 = 3000\n\\]\n\\[\n50 \\times 5 = 250\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times 60 = 480\n\\]\n\\[\n8 \\times 5 = 40\n\\]\n\nAdding all these results together:\n\n\\[\n3000 + 250 + 480 + 40\n\\]\n\nFirst, add \\(3000 + 250\\):\n\n\\[\n3000 + 250 = 3250\n\\]\n\nNext, add \\(3250 + 480\\):\n\n\\[\n3250 + 480 = 3730\n\\]\n\nFinally, add \\(3730 + 40\\):\n\n\\[\n3730 + 40 = 3770\n\\]\n\nTo confirm, we can use the standard vertical multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   58 \\\\\n\\times 65 \\\\\n\\hline\n  290 \\quad (\\text{58} \\times 5) \\\\\n+3480 \\quad (\\text{58} \\times 60) \\\\\n\\hline\n  3770 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 58 and 65 is \\(\\boxed{3770}\\).",
