Let me do the multiplication again:\n\n88 \u00d7 98.\n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\nFirst, 88 \u00d7 98.\n\nMultiply 88 by 8 (units digit):\n\n8 \u00d7 8 = 64, write down 4, carryover 6.\n\n8 \u00d7 8 = 64 + 6 = 70. So first partial product is 704.\n\nThen multiply 88 by 9 (tens digit, so actually 90, so the result is shifted left by one):\n\n9 \u00d7 8 = 72, write down 2, carryover 7.\n\n9 \u00d7 8 = 72 + 7 = 79. So the partial product is 792, but shifted left, so 7920.\n\nAdding 704 and 7920:\n\n704 + 7920.\n\nLet me add 700 + 7900 = 8600, then 4 + 20 = 24. Total is 8624.\n\nYes, so that's correct. So the correct answer is 8624, not 6624. The user might have made a mistake in their calculation, perhaps in the multiplication or addition steps. Maybe they thought 8\u00d79 is 63 instead of 72? Let's see:\n\nIf someone does 8\u00d79 as 63 (which is wrong, since 9\u00d77 is 63), then:\n\nFirst partial product would be 9\u00d788= 792 (if they did 9\u00d788 as 9\u00d780=720 +9\u00d78=72, totaling 792). Wait, but if they thought 9\u00d78 is 63, then 9\u00d788 would be 9\u00d780=720 + 9\u00d78=63 (incorrectly) totaling 720+63=783. Then the partial products would be 704 (from 8\u00d788) plus 7830 (since shifted left) would be 704 +7830= 8534. Still not 6624.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they messed up the first multiplication. Let's say they did 8\u00d78 as 64, then 8\u00d78 as 64, but added wrong. Like 8\u00d78 is first digit (units) 4, carryover 6. Then the next digit is 8\u00d78=64 plus carryover 6 is 70, so that's correct. So that gives 704. Then multiplying by 9, but if they did 9\u00d78 as 63 (thinking 7\u00d79=63), then 63, carryover 6. Then next digit 9\u00d78=72 +6=78, so the partial product would be 783, shifted to 7830. Then 704+7830=8534. Still not 6624.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 88\u00d798 as (90-2)\u00d7(90-2) which would be 90\u00b2 - 2\u00d790\u00d72 +2\u00b2? Wait no, that's (a-b)\u00b2 formula. (a-b)\u00b2= a\u00b2-2ab +b\u00b2