ing. \n\nIn any case, based on my calculations, the correct answer is 1716. The user's proposed answer of 1714 is incorrect. So I should explain that the correct product is 1716 and point out where the mistake might have happened.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 44 and 39 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step-by-Step Calculation:**\n1. **Break down using distributive property:**  \n   \\( (40 + 4) \\times (30 + 9) \\)  \n   \\( = 40 \\times 30 + 40 \\times 9 + 4 \\times 30 + 4 \\times 9 \\)\n\n2. **Compute each term:**  \n   - \\( 40 \\times 30 = 1200 \\)  \n   - \\( 40 \\times 9 = 360 \\)  \n   - \\( 4 \\times 30 = 120 \\)  \n   - \\( 4 \\times 9 = 36 \\)\n\n3. **Sum all terms:**  \n   \\( 1200 + 360 = 1560 \\)  \n   \\( 1560 + 120 = 1680 \\)  \n   \\( 1680 + 36 = \\boxed{1716} \\)\n\n**Verification via Standard Multiplication:**  \n```\n\u2003\u200344  \n\u00d7\u00a039  \n-------  \n\u2003396\u2003(44 \u00d7 9)  \n+1320\u2003(44 \u00d7 30, shifted left by 1 digit)  \n-------  \n\u20031716  \n```\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe correct answer is **1716**. The initial guess of 1714 is incorrect, likely due to an arithmetic error during addition or multiplication steps. Always double-check your calculations!",
