00\n\nThen 80\u00d76 = 480\n\nThen 3\u00d790 = 270\n\nThen 3\u00d76 =18\n\nNow add all these together: 7200 + 480 = 7680; 7680 +270 = 7950; 7950 +18=7968. Yep, same result. So that confirms it's 7968. So the user's answer of 7966 is incorrect, likely due to an arithmetic error in their calculation.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps check via another method. Let me try using the difference of squares or some other trick. Hmm, 83 and 96. Maybe if I can express them as numbers around a common midpoint?\n\nAlternatively, think of 83 \u00d7 96 as 83 \u00d7 (100 - 4) = 83\u00d7100 - 83\u00d74 = 8300 - 332. Let's compute that. 8300 - 300 is 8000, then subtract 32 more: 8000 -32 = 7968. Again, same answer. So definitely 7968. So the user must have made a mistake in their calculation. Let me see where they might have gone wrong.\n\nIf they thought the answer is 7966, perhaps in the addition step of the partial products they messed up. Let me see: If they had 498 + 7470. If they added 498 + 7470 incorrectly. Wait, 7470 + 498:\n\n7000 + 400 = 7400,\n\n400 + 400 = 800? Wait, maybe they added 498 + 7470 as:\n\n7470 + 400 = 7870,\n\nthen 7870 + 98 = 7968. But if they miscalculated 7470 + 498 as 7470 + 498, perhaps they added wrong. Maybe they thought 7470 + 400 is 7870, but then added 96 instead of 98? 7870 + 96 is 7966. Oh, that could be. So if they thought that the second number was 496 instead of 498, that would give 7966. So maybe they made an error in the first partial product.\n\nWait, let's see: If in the first partial product (6\u00d783), they might have miscalculated. Let me see: 6\u00d780 is 480, plus 6\u00d73 is 18, so 480 +18=498. If they instead did 6\u00d780=480, and then 6\u00d73=18, but added 480+10=490, forgetting the 8? That would give 490, then 490 + 7470=7960, but that's not 7966. Alternatively, maybe they thought 8\u00d76=54? Wait no, 8\u00d76 is 48. Wait, perhaps in the first step they thought 6\u00d78 is 48, plus carryover 1 (from the units digit?) Wait, no. Let me