  "choices": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "500 + 150 equals 650. Wait, but the user thought the answer was 150. That's way off. Maybe they only did 50 times 3 and forgot the 10 part? I should double-check my steps. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do it another way to confirm. Let me try the standard multiplication method. Writing 50 on top and 13 below. But since 50 is two digits and 13 is two digits, actually, maybe I need to line them up properly. Wait, actually, when multiplying 50 by 13, you can consider 13 as a two-digit number. So, multiply 50 by 3 first, which is 150. Then multiply 50 by 10 (since the 1 in 13 is in the tens place), so that's 50 times 10, which is 500. Then add those two results together: 150 + 500 is indeed 650. So that checks out. .\n\nAnother way to think about it: 13 times 50. Since multiplication is commutative, it's the same as 50 times 13. Maybe 13 times 50 is easier for some people. 13 times 10 is 130, so 13 times 50 is 130 times 5, which is 650. Yep, same answer. So the correct product should be 650, not 150. The user might have made a mistake by only multiplying 50 by 3 instead of considering both the tens and ones place in 13. I should explain that clearly so they understand where they went wrong. .\n</think>\n\nThe product of 50 and 13 is calculated by multiplying the two numbers together. Here's the step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Break down 13 into 10 + 3** (using the distributive property):\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 13 = 50 \\times (10 + 3)\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Multiply 50 by each part separately**:\n   \\[\n   50 \\times 10 = 500 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 50 \\times 3 = 150\n   \\]\n   \n3. **Add the results**:\n   \\[\n   500 + 150 = 650\n   \\]\n\n**Answer:** \\(\\boxed{650}\\)\n\nThe correct product of 50 and 13 is **650**, not 150. It looks like the mistake was only multiplying by the \"3\" in 13 and forgetting the \"10\" part. Always remember to account for all digits in the number! \ud83d\ude0a",
