>\n\nThe product of 62 and 19 can be calculated using the distributive property or standard multiplication. Here's a step-by-step breakdown:\n\n### Method 1: Distributive Property (Breakdown)\n1. **Express 19 as 20 - 1**:\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 19 = 62 \\times (20 - 1)\n   \\]\n2. **Calculate \\(62 \\times 20\\)**:\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 20 = 1,\\!240\n   \\]\n3. **Subtract \\(62 \\times 1\\) from the result**:\n   \\[\n   1,\\!240 - 62 = 1,\\!178\n   \\]\n\n### Method 2: Standard Multiplication\n1. **Multiply 62 by the units digit (9)**:\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 9 = 558\n   \\]\n2. **Multiply 62 by the tens digit (10)**:\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 10 = 620\n   \\]\n3. **Add the results**:\n   \\[\n   558 + 620 = 1,\\!178\n   \\]\n\n### Verification\nUsing another approach:\n\\[\n(60 + 2) \\times (20 - 1) = 60 \\times 20 - 60 \\times 1 + 2 \\times 20 - 2 \\times 1 = 1,\\!200 - 60 + 40 - 2 = 1,\\!178\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\nThe correct product is **1,178**. The initial thought of 1,174 likely resulted from a minor arithmetic error during calculation. Always double-check by using different methods to confirm consistency.\n\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{1178}\\)",
