heck that: 62\u00d7100=6200, 62\u00d738=2356, so total 6200+2356=8556. Close to 8550 but still not exactly. Maybe they thought 62\u00d725\u00d75.4? Not sure. Anyway, the correct answer is 1550. \n\nI should explain the steps clearly so that the user can see where they might have gone wrong. Maybe they miscounted the zeros or added incorrectly when doing the partial products. It's important to carefully check each step in multiplication to avoid errors.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 62 and 25 is calculated as follows:\n\n**Step-by-Step Explanation:**\n\n1. **Breakdown using the distributive property:**\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 25 = (60 + 2) \\times 25\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 60 \\times 25 + 2 \\times 25\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 1500 + 50 = 1550\n   \\]\n\n2. **Alternative method (standard algorithm):**\n   ```\n     62  \n   \u00d7 25  \n   ------  \n    310  (62 \u00d7 5)  \n  +1240  (62 \u00d7 20, shifted left by one digit)  \n  ------  \n    1550  \n   ```\n\n3. **Using the fact that 25 = 100/4:**\n   \\[\n   62 \\times 25 = \\frac{62 \\times 100}{4} = \\frac{6200}{4} = 1550\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nThe product of 62 and 25 is **1550**.\n\n---\n\n**Note:** The initial guess of 8550 is incorrect. Common mistakes might involve miscounting zeros or miscalculating partial products. Always double-check your work using different methods!",
