(60 - 1) \\times 65\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Expand the expression:**\n   \\[\n   = 60 \\times 65 - 1 \\times 65\n   \\]\n   \n3. **Calculate each term:**\n   - \\(60 \\times 65 = 3900\\)  \n     *(Because \\(60 \\times 60 = 3600\\) and \\(60 \\times 5 = 300\\), so \\(3600 + 300 = 3900\\)).*\n   - \\(1 \\times 65 = 65\\)\n   \n4. **Subtract the second term from the first:**\n   \\[\n   3900 - 65 = 3835\n   \\]\n\n**Verification via Standard Algorithm:**\n- Multiply \\(59 \\times 5\\) (units place):  \n  \\(5 \\times 9 = 45\\), carryover 4; \\(5 \\times 5 = 25 + 4 = 29\\).  \n  Partial product: **295**.\n  \n- Multiply \\(59 \\times 60\\) (tens place, represented by the \"6\" in 65):  \n  \\(6 \\times 9 = 54\\), carryover 5; \\(6 \\times 5 = 30 + 5 = 35\\).  \n  Partial product: **3540** (shifted one place to the left).\n\n- Add the partial products:  \n  \\[\n  295 + 3540 = 3835\n  \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe correct product of 59 and 65 is **3835**. The initial answer of 3895 likely resulted from an arithmetic error (e.g., miscalculating \\(59 \\times 5\\) as 355 instead of 295). Always double-check intermediate steps to avoid such mistakes!\n\n<\uff5cFinal Answer\uff5c> The product of 59 and 65 is \\boxed{3835}.",
