30, 7\u00d77 is 49, so total 630+49=679. So that's correct. Therefore the step-by-step method must be wrong. Wait, but how?\n\nAh! Wait, I think I see the mistake. When multiplying by the 7 (units place), the second digit is the tens place of the original number (97), so 7\u00d79 (tens place) is indeed 63, plus the carryover 4 gives 67, so the total is 67 (hundreds and tens place) plus the 9 (units). Wait, no, actually, the 67 is in the tens and hundreds place. So 673 is the correct result? But according to the breakdown, it should be 679. Wait, this is very confusing. Let me just compute it manually.\n\n97 \u00d77:\n\nLet me compute 100\u00d77=700. Then subtract 3\u00d77=21, so 700-21=679. So that's correct. Therefore the step-by-step must have an error.\n\nWait, so when I did the step-by-step, I thought that 7\u00d79 (the tens digit) was 63 plus carryover 4, which gives 67, so writing down 67 in the tens and hundreds place, so total 673. But according to the other method, it's 679. So where is the mistake?\n\nWait, the problem is that when you write down 67 in the tens and hundreds place, that would be 670, plus the 3= 673. But according to the correct value, it should be 679. Therefore, there must be an error in the step-by-step calculation.\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying 7 (the units digit of the multiplier) by the tens digit of the multiplicand (9), that gives 63, plus the carryover 4 (from the units place), so 63 +4 =67. So the 67 is the value for the tens place and higher. So the 7 in the tens place (from 67) would be in the tens column, and the 6 would be in the hundreds column. So that's 6 (hundreds), 7 (tens), and 9 (units) = 679. Wait, hold on, that is different from what I thought before. Wait, perhaps I made a mistake in writing the digits.\n\nLet me write it out in columns:\n\n       97\n\n     \u00d7  7\n\n     -----\n\nStarting from the rightmost digit:\n\n7 (units) \u00d77 (units digit of 97) =49. Write down 9 in the units place, carryover 4 to the tens place.\n\nThen, 7 \u00d79 (tens digit of 97) =63. Add the carried over 4: 63+4=67. So the 67 is written in the tens and hundreds place. So that's 6 (hundreds) and 7 (tens). So altogether, the result is 679. \n\nAh! I see now. I must have mistakenly written the 67 as being in the tens and hundreds place but thought it was 67 in the tens place only, hence 670 +9. But actually, 67 is 6 hundreds and 7 tens, so 670, plus the 9 gives 679. Wait, no, actually, the 67 is already the value after adding the carryover. So the 67 is the total for the tens and higher places. Therefore, the total is 670 +9 =679. Wait, but actually, the way it's written:\n\nWhen you write down the partial product, after multiplying by the units digit, you have:\n\nFirst digit (units): 9\n\nThen, when moving to the tens digit:\n\n7\u00d79=63 +4=67, so you write 7 in the tens place and carryover 6 to the hundreds place. Wait, but actually, you have to place it properly.\n\nLet me write it as:\n\n       97\n\n     \u00d7  7\n\n     -----\n\n       679\n\nBecause:\n\nFirst, 7\u00d77=49, write 9, carryover 4.\n\nThen 7\u00d79=63 +4=67, so write 7 in the tens place and 6 in the hundreds place. Therefore, it's 6 in hundreds, 7 in tens, and 9 in units: 679. Yes! So my earlier mistake was in thinking that the 67 was in the tens place, but actually, it's split into hundreds and tens. Therefore, the correct partial product is 679, not 673. That was the error. So that explains the discrepancy.\n\nTherefore, going back to the standard multiplication method:\n\nFirst partial product (units digit multiplication): 97\u00d77=679.\n\nSecond partial product (tens digit, so 30's place):\n\n97\u00d730. To compute 97\u00d73\u00d710. 97\u00d73: let's compute that.\n\n97\u00d73:\n\n3\u00d77=21, write 1, carryover 2.\n\n3\u00d79=27 +2=29. So 291. Then times 10 is 2910.\n\nTherefore, the second partial product is 2910.\n\nNow, adding the two partial products: 2910 + 679.\n\nLet me add them step