ith calculator steps. Since I don't have a calculator here, but let's compute 97\u00d737:\n\nFirst, 100\u00d737=3700, minus 3\u00d737=111, so 3700-111=3589. That's the first method.\n\nAlternatively, 97\u00d737:\n\nBreak down 97 as 90 +7, and 37 as 30+7.\n\nSo (90+7)(30+7) =90\u00d730 +90\u00d77 +7\u00d730 +7\u00d77.\n\nCompute each term:\n\n90\u00d730=2700,\n\n90\u00d77=630,\n\n7\u00d730=210,\n\n7\u00d77=49.\n\nAdding all together: 2700+630=3330; 210+49=259; 3330+259=3589. So that's another confirmation.\n\nTherefore, after correcting the earlier mistake in the multiplication steps, it's clear that the correct product is 3589. The user initially thought 3584, which is incorrect. The error likely came from an incorrect partial product in the multiplication steps.\n\nTherefore, the answer is 3589.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe product of 97 and 37 is \\boxed{3589}.\n</think>\n\nTo find the product of 97 and 37, we can use the distributive property by breaking down 97 into (100 - 3):\n\n\\[\n97 \\times 37 = (100 - 3) \\times 37\n\\]\n\nApplying the distributive property:\n\n\\[\n(100 - 3) \\times 37 = 100 \\times 37 - 3 \\times 37\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term separately:\n\n\\[\n100 \\times 37 = 3700\n\\]\n\\[\n3 \\times 37 = 111\n\\]\n\nSubtracting the second result from the first:\n\n\\[\n3700 - 111 = 3589\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we can use the standard multiplication method:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{r}\n   97 \\\\\n\\times 37 \\\\\n\\hline\n   679 \\quad \\text{(97 \u00d7 7)} \\\\\n+2910 \\quad \\text{(97 \u00d7 30)} \\\\\n\\hline\n  3589 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nBoth methods confirm that the product of 97 and 37 is \\(\\boxed{3589}\\).",
