 follows:\n\n**Method 1: Using the 11s multiplication trick**  \nFor a two-digit number \\( ab \\), multiplying by 11 gives \\( a(a + b)b \\).  \nHere, \\( a = 1 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\).  \n- Add the digits: \\( 1 + 3 = 4 \\).  \n- Place the sum between the digits: \\( 1 \\, 4 \\, 3 \\).  \nResult: \\( 143 \\).\n\n**Method 2: Traditional multiplication**  \n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n13 \\times 11 &= 13 \\times (10 + 1) \\\\\n&= 13 \\times 10 + 13 \\times 1 \\\\\n&= 130 + 13 \\\\\n&= 143.\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe correct answer is **143**, not 148. It looks like there was an arithmetic error in the initial guess. Always double-check using different methods to ensure accuracy!  \n\n\\(\\boxed{143}\\)",
