it left (because it's the tens place), they forgot to shift, so it's 492. Then adding the first partial product 82 gives 574. Still not 3002. \n\nAlternatively, if they multiplied 82\u00d761 as 82\u00d7(60+1) and miscalculated 82\u00d760 as 3000. For instance, 82\u00d76 is 492, so 82\u00d760 is 4920, but if they thought 82\u00d760 is 3000, then adding 82 gives 3082. Still not 3002. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 82\u00d760 is 82\u00d750 + 82\u00d710. 82\u00d750 is 4100, 82\u00d710=820, so total is 4100+820=4920. Then adding 82 gives 5002. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 80\u00d760=4800 and 2\u00d71=2, then 4800 +2=4802. But that's missing the cross terms. \n\nWait, 3002 is exactly half of 6004. Hmm, but 82\u00d761 is 5002, so not sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 82\u00d761 is (80+2)(60+1)= (80\u00d760)+(80\u00d71)+(2\u00d760)+(2\u00d71)=4800+80+120+2=5002. So that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 82 multiplied by 37, which is 3034, but rounded down or something. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 82\u00d737= (80\u00d730)+(80\u00d77)+(2\u00d730)+(2\u00d77)=2400+560+60+14=2400+560=2960+60=3020+14=3034. Close to 3002 but still not. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 82\u00d761 is 82\u00d7(60+1)=82\u00d760 +82\u00d71= but messed up 82\u00d760 as 3000. 3000+82=3082. Not 3002.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 82\u00d760 is 4920, but divided by 2, getting 2460, then added 542? No idea. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they added 82+61=143, then multiplied by 21? 143\u00d721=3003. Oh! Wait, that's close to 3002. Maybe that's it. If they thought the question was the sum multiplied by something? But the question says \"product of 82 and 61\". But if someone misreads it as (82+61)\u00d7something, like (82+61)=143, and then multiplied by 21, getting 143\u00d721=3003, which is close to 3002. Maybe they thought it was 21 instead of 61. \n\nAlternatively, if they did 82\u00d737, which is 3034, but thought 37 is 36 and miscalculated. \n\nAlternativ