\u00d78=48 plus 1 is 49. So the partial product is 492. But shifted left, it's 4920. So adding 82 gives 5002. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps they thought the 6 was in the units place and the 1 in the tens, so multiplying 82\u00d716 instead? 82\u00d716=1312. No. \n\nAlternatively, if they flipped the numbers and did 61\u00d782, but that's the same as 82\u00d761. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 82\u00d761 is 82\u00d76\u00d710 + 82\u00d76\u00d71? Not sure. \n\nHmm. I'm going around in circles here. The correct answer is definitely 5002. The user might have made an arithmetic mistake in their calculation. So, I should explain the correct steps and point out where they might have gone wrong. \n\nWait, let me check 82\u00d761 using another method. Let me do 82\u00d761:\n\nBreakdown:\n\n82 \u00d7 61 = 82 \u00d7 (60 + 1) = (82 \u00d7 60) + (82 \u00d7 1) = 4920 + 82 = 5002. \n\nAlternatively, using the standard algorithm:\n\n\u2003\u200382  \n\u00d7\u00a061  \n------  \n\u2003\u200382  (82 \u00d7 1)  \n+4920 (82 \u00d7 60)  \n------  \n\u20035002  \n\nYes, that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user subtracted instead of added? 4920 -82=4838. Not 3002. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 82 by 37? 82\u00d737=3034. Not 3002. \n\nAlternatively, 82\u00d736=2952. Hmm. \n\nAlternatively, 82\u00d737 minus 32 is 3002. 3034-32=3002. But why would they do that? \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user did 82\u00d737, but thought 37\u00d72=74, so 74\u00d7 something. Not sure. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 82\u00d73.7\u00d7something? Not likely. \n\nAlternatively, perhaps they had a calculator error, like entering 82\u00d761 as 82\u00d761 but the calculator was in some other mode? Unlikely. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they added all the digits: 8+2+6+1=17, then multiplied by something? 17\u00d7177=3009. Not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 8\u00d76=48, 2\u00d71=2, then 48\u00d72=96. No. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 82\u00d761 as (80+2)*(60+1)=80*60 + 80*1 +