      "text": "2790? Wait, but the user thought it was 7790. That's way bigger. Hmm, maybe I did something wrong here.\n\nWait, maybe I should try another method to verify. Let's do the standard multiplication. Write 90 times 31.\n\nSo, writing it out vertically:\n\n\u2003\u200390  \n\u00d731  \n------  \nFirst multiply 90 by 1, which is 90.  \nThen multiply 90 by 30 (since the 3 is in the tens place), so that's 90 \u00d7 30 = 2700.  \nThen add them together: 90 + 2700 = 2790.  \n\nHmm, so that's the same result as before. But the user thinks it's 7790. That's a big difference. Maybe there was a typo in the question? Or maybe I misunderstood the question? Let me check again.\n\nWait, 90 \u00d7 31. Let me try multiplying step by step. \n\nAlternatively, maybe I can do 90 \u00d7 31 as (9 \u00d7 10) \u00d7 (31). So 9\u00d731 is 279, then times 10 is 2790. Yeah, that's the same answer. \n\nAlternatively, 31 \u00d7 90. Let me do it another way. 30 times 90 is 2700, then 1 times 90 is 90, so adding them gives 2790. \n\nHmm, so why does the user think it's 7790? Maybe they added an extra digit somewhere? Like maybe they did 90 \u00d7 31 as 9 \u00d7 31 = 279 and then multiplied by 10 again? 279 \u00d710 is 2790, but if they thought 90 is 9\u00d710, then maybe they thought 9\u00d731\u00d710 = 279 \u00d710 = 2790, but that's right. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought 90 \u00d731 is 9\u00d73=27 and then 9\u00d71=9, but that's not the way. Wait, perhaps they did 90 \u00d730 is 2700, then 90 \u00d71 is 90, but then added 2700 + 900? Maybe a miscalculation. Wait 2700 + 900 would be 3600, but that's not 7790. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 90 \u00d7 31 as (9\u00d73) which is 27, then (0\u00d71)=0, and then added 27 and 0? That doesn't make sense. Wait, perhaps they did 90 \u00d731 as 90 \u00d7 (30 +1) but then 90\u00d730=2700 and 90\u00d71=90, but added 2700+900? Wait, where would the 900 come from? \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 90 \u00d7 31 is 9\u00d731 \u