ithmetic error when adding or multiplying. Let me check once more step by step.\n\nCalculating 17 \u00d7 55:\n\nMethod 1:\n17 \u00d7 55 = 17 \u00d7 (50 +5) = (17\u00d750) + (17\u00d75)\n17\u00d750: 17\u00d75=85, so 85\u00d710=850\n17\u00d75=85\nAdding them together: 850 +85=935. \n\nMethod 2:\nUsing the standard algorithm:\n   17\n\u00d7 55\n-------\n   85  (17\u00d75)\n+850  (17\u00d750, shifted one position to the left)\n-------\n  935\n\nMethod 3:\nBreakdown:\n17 \u00d755 = (20 -3) \u00d755 = 20\u00d755 -3\u00d755 = 1100 -165 = 935.\n\nAll methods confirm that the correct product is 935, not 931. The user might have made a mistake in their calculation. Perhaps they thought 17\u00d75 is 85, but then added 850 +85 and got 935, but then maybe subtracted 4 by mistake? Not sure. Anyway, the correct answer is 935.\n</think>\n\nThe product of 17 and 55 is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Breakdown Method**:\n   - Multiply 17 by 50: \\(17 \\times 50 = 850\\).\n   - Multiply 17 by 5: \\(17 \\times 5 = 85\\).\n   - Add the results: \\(850 + 85 = 935\\).\n\n2. **Standard Algorithm**:\n   - Multiply 17 by 5 (ones place): \\(17 \\times 5 = 85\\).\n   - Multiply 17 by 50 (tens place, shifted left): \\(17 \\times 50 = 850\\).\n   - Sum the partial products: \\(85 + 850 = 935\\).\n\n3. **Verification via Distributive Property**:\n   - \\(17 \\times 55 = (20 - 3) \\times 55 = 20 \\times 55 - 3 \\times 55 = 1100 - 165 = 935\\).\n\nAll methods confirm the correct answer is **935**. The initial thought of 931 likely contains an arithmetic error. \n\n**Final Answer:** \\boxed{935}",
