messages:  [['Please solve the math problem.\nQuestion: What is the Product of 73 and 91?. I think the correct answer is: 6640\n', "<think>:.\nOkay, so I need to find the product of 73 and 91. Hmm, let me think. Multiplying two numbers.. I remember that multiplication is just repeated addition, but doing that here would take too long. Maybe I can use the standard algorithm for multiplication? Let me recall how that works.\n\nFirst, I should write down the two numbers, one on top of the other. Let me see, 73 and 91. It might be easier to have the larger number on top, but actually, it doesn't really matter because multiplication is commutative. So either way, the result should be the same. Let me put 91 on top and 73 below. Wait, actually, maybe 73 is a bit simpler because it's a two-digit number. Let me try arranging it:.\n\n      73.\n    x 91.\n    -----.\n\nAlright, so the standard method is to multiply each digit of the bottom number by each digit of the top number, starting from the right. Let me start with the units place. The bottom number is 73, so the digits are 3 (units) and 7 (tens). The top number is 91, so 1 (units) and 9 (tens). Wait, actually, I think I might have mixed up the order. Let me make sure.\n\nWait, actually, when using the standard algorithm, you usually write the larger number on top, but in this case, both are two-digit numbers. Let me just proceed step by step.\n\nFirst, multiply 73 by the units digit of 91, which is 1. So 73 times 1 is 73. Then, multiply 73 by the tens digit of 91, which is 9. But since 9 is in the tens place, it's actually 90. So that would be 73 times 90. Then, add those two results together.\n\nWait, let me write it out properly:.\n\n        73.\n      x 91.\n      ------.\n        73   (this is 73 x 1).\n      +6570  (this is 73 x 90, shifted one position to the left).\n      -------.\n      ?.\n\nWait, hold on, maybe I should do it step by step. Let me try again.\n\nStarting with the units digit of the bottom number. Wait, actually, in the standard algorithm, you usually take the bottom number's digits. Let me check again.\n\nIf the problem is 73 multiplied by 91, you can arrange it as:.\n\n       91.\n     x 73.\n     -----.\n\nBut maybe it's easier to have the numbers as 73 times 91. Let me pick the way where the second number is the one you break down. Let me see, perhaps breaking down 91 into 90 + 1, so 73*(90 +1) = 73*90 +73*1. That might be easier.\n\nCalculating 73*90: Well, 73*9 is 657, so 73*90 would be 6570. Then 73*1 is 73. Adding them together: 6570 +73. Let me add those. Therefore, my final answer is: "]]
