ite 4, carryover1; hundreds:2+4=6 +1=7; thousands:3. So total is 3741.\n\nHmm. So clearly, the correct answer is 3741, but the user says they think it's 3771. Maybe they made a mistake in their calculation. Perhaps they thought 8\u00d74=32 and then added the wrong digits.\n\nWait, maybe they did 80\u00d740=3200, 80\u00d73=240, 7\u00d740=280, 7\u00d73=21, then added 3200+240=3440, then 280+21=301, then 3440+301=3741. Still the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 87\u00d743 as (85+2)\u00d743, which would be 85\u00d743 +2\u00d743. 85\u00d740=3400, 85\u00d73=255, so 3400+255=3655, plus 86 (2\u00d743=86) gives 3655+86=3741. Still same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they added wrong in their head. Let me think, if someone does 87\u00d740=3480, and 87\u00d73=261. Then adding 3480+261, perhaps they thought 3480 +260=3740, then +1=3741. But if they instead thought 3480+261 as 3480 +200=3680, then +60=3740, then +1=3741. So that's correct. So where would 3771 come from?\n\nWait, 3771 minus 3741 is 30. So someone might have added an extra 30 somewhere. Maybe when multiplying 4\u00d78, they thought 4\u00d78 is 32, plus a carryover that didn't exist? Let me see the partial products again.\n\nFirst partial product: 3\u00d787=261.\n\nSecond partial product: 4\u00d787 shifted left by one digit, so 4\u00d787=348, so shifted becomes 3480.\n\nAdding 261+3480: 261+3480. Let me see, if someone miscalculates 3480 + 261 as 3480 + 200=3680, then adds 60 more to get 3740, then adds 1 to get 3741. But if they added 3480 + 260, that's 3740, then added 11 instead of 1, getting 3751? Hmm, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe in the second partial product, they thought 4\u00d78 is 32, plus a carryover of 2 (from the previous digit?) but actually, when multiplying 4\u00d78, there's no carryover yet. Wait, let me re-examine the second partial product step.\n\nSecond partial product: 4 (from 43) times 87.\n\nStarting from the right, 4\u00d77 is 28, write down 8, carryover 2.\n\nThen 4\u00d78 is 32, plus 